 "title": "Supply Chain Interview Question on Diet Coke",
 "topic": "supply chain",
 "summary": "This video shows an interview scenario where a candidate is asked how to distribute 10,000 extra bottles of Diet Coke in Bangalore when India plays Pakistan in the finals today. The candidate chooses to send all bottles to the top 20 stores to maximize sales, but the interviewer points out that the remaining 80 stores lose customers.",
 "key_points": [
  "The scenario involves planning the supply of Diet Coke in Bangalore with 10,000 extra bottles and 100 total stores.",
  "The candidate chooses to send all 10,000 bottles to the top 20 stores to sell faster where it sells faster.",
  "The interviewer highlights the downside: customers at the remaining 80 stores cannot find Diet Coke and the match happens today with no tomorrow.",
  "The creator claims that sending to only top 20 stores maximizes sales but causes the other 80 stores to lose customers."
 ],
 "content": "POV: The last question before the 42LPA Offer 😨\n\nSo, imagine you are the supply chain manager for Coca Cola. Okay? So, India is playing Pakistan in the finals today, and you need to plan the supply of diet coke, got it? So, now you have 10,000 extra bottles of diet coke in Bangalore. Make sense? And you have two options: send all the 10,000 to your top 20 stores in Bangalore, or distribute them across all your 100 stores in Bangalore. Understood? What would you choose and why?\n\nSir, I'll send them all to the top 20 stores.\n\nOkay, so you send all 10,000 diet coke cans to your top 20 stores, right? Because sir, it will sell faster and I want to keep inventory where it will sell faster.\n\nOkay fine, let's assume that you send them to the 20 stores. Now the match is starting, and now all the diet coke is sold out in the 20 stores.\n\nOh, okay sir. But the customers in the rest of the 80 stores are making an issue because they cannot find diet coke.\n\nSir, we can always send more tomorrow.\n\nTomorrow? The match is happening today and there's no tomorrow.\n\nYes sir, that makes sense.\n\nSo, by sending those 10,000 units to your top 20 stores, you did maximize sales, but the remaining 80 stores are losing customers. Is that the right decision?\n\nYes sir, got your point. Unfortunately, that was not the approach I was looking for, but better luck next time.\n\nOkay sir, thanks for the feedback, I'll work harder next time.\n\nDrop a comment on how you would have answered! 🙂\n\nComment \"Framework\" and I'll share the exact framework to solve such questions!\nYour last question before the 42LPA offer be like 😄 I'll be posting the answer video in a few days till then please comment what you would have answered and I'll pick the best one!\n\n#interview #supplychain #finance #marketingdigital\n\nText on screen: POV: The last question before the 42LPA Offer 😨",
